  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how proteins can move from normal functional states into amyloid-like aggregates and how these assemblies are organized across length scales. The top schematic shows several interconverting protein states, including native, unfolded, membrane-bound, functional complex, and liquid condensate. From these states, proteins can enter a primary nucleation pathway that produces oligomers, then fibrils, which grow by elongation and can be amplified by fragmentation. A secondary nucleation route appears to let existing fibrils seed the formation of additional oligomers and fibrils, highlighting a self-propagating aggregation cycle. The diagram emphasizes that multiple starting conformations can feed into this process rather than a single linear route.\n\nThe lower part illustrates the structural hierarchy of the resulting material. At the smallest scale, a cross-β-sheet motif forms the basic molecular architecture. These sheets stack into a filament, filaments associate into fibril bundles, and bundles or tangled aggregates accumulate into larger plaques. A scale bar progression indicates growth from nanometer-sized molecular structure to micrometer fibrils and bundles, up to millimeter-scale deposits. Overall, the figure links protein misfolding pathways with the multiscale architecture of amyloid assemblies.",
